// ALG1 Unit 06 Lesson 03 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Modeling with Systems
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.AR.6; MLA.A1.AR.7
::ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.6
Question:
A store sells pens for $2 and notebooks for $5. Which equation models spending exactly $30, where p is pens and n is notebooks?
{
~A. 5p + 2n \= 30#This reverses the prices for pens and notebooks.
=B. 2p + 5n \= 30#Each pen contributes 2 dollars and each notebook contributes 5 dollars.
~C. p + n \= 30#This counts items, not total cost.
~D. 2p + 5n <\= 30#This models spending at most 30 dollars, not exactly 30 dollars.
}

::ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.7
Question:
For the constraint 4x + 10y >= 40, does (5, 2) satisfy the requirement?
{
~A. No, because 5 + 2 is less than 40#The point values must be weighted by 4 and 10.
~B. No, because 4(5) + 10(2) \= 20#The arithmetic is incomplete; 20 + 20 = 40.
~C. No, because equality is not allowed#The symbol >= includes equality.
=D. Yes, because 4(5) + 10(2) \= 40#The total equals 40, and equality satisfies at least 40.
}

::ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.6
Question:
A club can buy no more than 18 total shirts and hats. If s is shirts and h is hats, which inequality represents the limit?
{
=A. s + h <\= 18#No more than 18 means the total is less than or equal to 18.
~B. s + h >\= 18#This means at least 18, not no more than 18.
~C. sh <\= 18#Multiplying the variables does not count total items.
~D. s - h <\= 18#Subtracting does not represent total items.
}

::ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.7
Question:
In a model, x represents small boxes and y represents large boxes. What does the ordered pair (6, 3) mean?
{
~A. 6 large boxes and 3 small boxes#This reverses the variable definitions.
~B. 9 total boxes only#The total may be 9, but the ordered pair gives more specific information.
=C. 6 small boxes and 3 large boxes#The x-coordinate matches small boxes and the y-coordinate matches large boxes.
~D. 3 small boxes and 6 large boxes#This reverses the coordinates.
}

::ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.7
Question:
A solution is called feasible when it:
{
~A. satisfies only the first constraint#A feasible solution must satisfy all constraints.
=B. satisfies every constraint in the model#Feasible means the solution works for the full system.
~C. has the largest possible x-value#A feasible point does not have to maximize x.
~D. makes both variables equal#Equal variable values are not required.
}

